    "prompt": "Function `RequestHandler.finish_response` in aiohttp/web_protocol.py. The statements below were written about this exact version of the function.\n\nFunction source (docstring removed):\n```python\nasync def finish_response(\n        self, request: BaseRequest, resp: StreamResponse, start_time: float\n    ) -> Tuple[StreamResponse, bool]:\n        request._finish()\n        if self._request_parser is not None:\n            self._request_parser.set_upgraded(False)\n            self._upgrade = False\n            if self._message_tail:\n                self._request_parser.feed_data(self._message_tail)\n                self._message_tail = b\"\"\n        try:\n            prepare_meth = resp.prepare\n        except AttributeError:\n            if resp is None:\n                self.log_exception(\"Missing return statement on request handler\")  # type: ignore[unreachable]\n            else:\n                self.log_exception(\n                    \"Web-handler should return a response instance, \"\n                    \"got {!r}\".format(resp)\n                )\n            exc = HTTPInternalServerError()\n            resp = Response(\n                status=exc.status, reason=exc.reason, text=exc.text, headers=exc.headers\n            )\n            prepare_meth = resp.prepare\n        try:\n            await prepare_meth(request)\n            await resp.write_eof()\n        except ConnectionError:\n            await self.log_access(request, resp, start_time)\n            return resp, True\n\n        await self.log_access(request, resp, start_time)\n        return resp, False\n```\n\nFacts extracted from this version by a parser (tree-sitter):\nsignature: finish_response(self, request, resp, start_time) -> Tuple[StreamResponse, bool]\ncalls: HTTPInternalServerError, Response, a response instance, \"\n                    \"got {!r}\".format, prepare_meth, request._finish, resp.write_eof, self._request_parser.feed_data, self._request_parser.set_upgraded, self.log_access, self.log_exception\nreturn statements: return resp, True | return resp, False\nraise statements: (none)\ncontrol flow: if=3 loops=0 try=2 with=0 returns=2 yields=0 awaits=8\n\n\nStatements:\nS1. `finish_response` calls `request._finish()` to modify the state of the request object.\nS2. `resp` is an instance of `StreamResponse`.\nS3. `finish_response` handles `AttributeError` by logging an exception message and retrying with a new `Response` instance.\nS4. `finish_response` is an asynchronous method in the `RequestHandler` class.\nS5. RequestHandler.finish_response returns True if the client disconnects prematurely.\nS6. RequestHandler.finish_response must be called within the context of any exception.\nS7. `finish_response` returns a tuple containing the response object and a boolean indicating whether the client disconnected prematurely.\nS8. `finish_response` handles `ConnectionError` by logging access information and returning the response with `True`, indicating client disconnection.\nS9. `finish_response` logs exceptions using `self.log_exception()`.\nS10. `request` is an instance of `BaseRequest`.\nS11. `finish_response` logs access information using `self.log_access()`.\nS12. `finish_response` writes the end-of-file marker using `write_eof` after preparing the response.\nS13. `finish_response` takes three parameters: `request`, `resp`, and `start_time`.\nS14. The access logger can get exception information if RequestHandler.finish_response is called within an exception context.\nS15. RequestHandler.finish_response prepares the response and writes EOF.\nS16. RequestHandler.finish_response logs access after preparing the response and writing EOF.\nS17. `start_time` is a float representing when the request was started.\n\nFor each statement decide:\n- supported: the shown code makes the statement true (a statement about purpose/intent counts as supported unless the code contradicts it).\n- contradicted: the shown code clearly makes the stateme